---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/site-layout.astro';

const title = 'LLC vs S-Corp: Which Tax Setup Makes Sense? | LLCAtlas';
const description =
  'Plain-English LLC vs S-corp comparison for solo founders: what changes, what stays the same, and when the S-corp election is worth discussing.';
const pageUrl = 'https://llcatlas.com/llc-vs-s-corp/';
const lastUpdatedIso = '2026-05-22';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'LLC vs S-Corp',
    description,
    author: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'LLCAtlas',
      url: 'https://llcatlas.com',
    },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'LLCAtlas',
      url: 'https://llcatlas.com',
    },
    datePublished: lastUpdatedIso,
    dateModified: lastUpdatedIso,
    mainEntityOfPage: {
      '@type': 'WebPage',
      '@id': pageUrl,
    },
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: 'https://llcatlas.com/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'LLC vs S-corp',
        item: pageUrl,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} ogType="article" structuredData={structuredData}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-8 lg:py-12">
    <header class="space-y-4">
      <p class="llc-helper">Entity comparison</p>
      <h1>LLC vs S-corp: the short version</h1>
      <p class="llc-section-copy text-ink-soft">
        An LLC is a legal entity. An S-corp is usually a tax election layered on top of an LLC. For
        most solo founders, the real question is not "LLC or S-corp?" It is "Should my LLC stay
        simple for now, or should I add payroll and S-corp tax treatment?"
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-10 llc-card-highlight space-y-5">
      <p class="llc-helper">Recommendation first</p>
      <h2>Start with the LLC. Consider S-corp treatment only after the income is consistent.</h2>
      <p class="llc-section-copy text-ink-soft">
        If you are under roughly $45K in net business income, S-corp treatment is usually premature.
        Around $60K+ and stable, the tax savings can start clearing payroll, accounting, and state
        costs. Use the calculator before you pay anyone to set it up.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row">
        <a
          href="/s-corp/election-calculator/"
          class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-accent px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-strong transition hover:bg-accent/85"
        >
          Run the S-corp calculator
        </a>
        <a
          href="/best-llc-services/"
          class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-rule-strong px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-strong transition hover:border-ink-muted hover:bg-surface"
        >
          Compare LLC services
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 space-y-5">
      <h2>What changes with S-corp treatment</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink-strong">You add payroll</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            The owner pays themselves a reasonable W-2 salary. Payroll tax applies to that salary,
            but remaining profit can be distributed without self-employment tax.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink-strong">You add compliance</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            More tax forms, more bookkeeping discipline, payroll deposits, and more CPA review. The
            savings have to beat the extra work.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink-strong">You keep the LLC wrapper</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            In the common solo-founder setup, the LLC remains the legal entity with the state. The
            S-corp election changes how the IRS taxes it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink-strong">You need a defensible salary</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            The IRS does not require a magic percentage, but it does expect reasonable compensation
            for the actual work the owner performs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 space-y-5">
      <h2>Related next steps</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <a href="/s-corp/reasonable-compensation/" class="llc-card transition hover:border-rule-strong">
          <span class="font-semibold text-ink-strong">Reasonable compensation calculator</span>
          <span class="mt-2 block text-sm leading-6 text-ink-soft">Pressure-test the salary assumption before you rely on the savings.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/self-employment-tax/calculator/" class="llc-card transition hover:border-rule-strong">
          <span class="font-semibold text-ink-strong">Self-employment tax calculator</span>
          <span class="mt-2 block text-sm leading-6 text-ink-soft">See the default LLC tax baseline first.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
